**The following are extremely relevant and important instructions. It would behoove you to follow them.**

### General Instructions

When creating plans in planning mode, do not make a "plan to make a plan". All planning should be done in the planning stage.

We like titles like `Example Title` in this case instead of `Example title`.

External repos are provided for you in the `external sources/` directory. This folder is read-only. If you need to reference the source code of a library please check here before fetching GitHub.

I really don't care about Mermaid diagrams. You don't need to create them in plans.

### Backend and Frontend Instructions

The backend venv is at src/backend/venv

Always use top-level imports in Python, not lazy/local/function level!
Do not re-export modules or use __all__. Instead, do direct imports.

Run tests with src/tests/run-tests.sh. We have like 2000+ tests so do not blindly run this script. Instead, run specific tests. More instructions available at src/tests/README.md.
YES: `./run-tests.sh test_api/test_app_releases.py` NO: `src/tests/run-tests.sh src/tests/test_api/test_app_releases.py`

Don't pile Django routes in the base views.py file. Instead, create new files to organize them in a manageable way.

You do not need custom try/catch logic for the sole purpose of logging errors. Our Django site has built-in error logging and will automatically generate 500 code responses.
All try/catch logging needs to explicitly print the full traceback. DO NOT swallow the exception and hide it!!

Prioritize using the provided Heroicons components for icons on the frontend instead of creating custom SVGs.
For example `import { ChevronDownIcon, Bars3Icon, XMarkIcon } from '@heroicons/vue/24/{outline or solid}';`

Make sure to explicitly define responses via pydantic for the backend and typescript for the frontend.

We have a bunch of common frontend components in the `parts/` folder. Please use those where possible.

DO NOT MANUALLY CREATE OR EDIT DJANGO MIGRATIONS!!!!! Use `manage.py` to generate them.

### Android Instructions

`android-common` has a lot of components and code that you should use across all apps. Before making any changes check what it provides. It's very important to correctly leverage `android-common` so that our apps remain consistent. `android-common-maps` provides a map component that handles all the usual logic and management.

Apps only target the latest Android version and SDK so do not do things like `if (Build.VERSION.SDK_INT >= Build.VERSION_CODES.O)`.

YOU ARE NOT ALLOWED TO DO `@Suppress("DEPRECATION")`!!!!!! YOU MUST FIX IT!!!!!!!

We are using Material Design 2 only. We have preset colors in `android-common`. DO NOT hard-code colors! No shadows on anything.

Additional code style instructions are in `src/android-tracker/CODE_STYLE.md`. These are tracker app-specific but good pointers for general Android development.

DO NOT FALL BACK TO DESTRUCTIVE DB MIGRATIONS!!!!!!!!!!!!! YOU MUST MIGRATE THEM CORRECTLY!!!!!!!!

We have a few external apps symlinked into the `src/` directory that may not show up correctly in your tools or in git. Use `ls` to find them. List:
- `android-survey-data-viewer`
